UNIT dequal
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG dequal(foo: any, bar: any) -> boolean
DEPENDS-ON none   # zero runtime dependencies; uses only Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty + built-in ctors

# =============================================================================
# OVERVIEW
# =============================================================================
# dequal is a pure, recursive deep-equality predicate. It returns a STRICT
# boolean (the literal `true` or `false`, never a truthy/falsy proxy). It has
# no seam (no clock/RNG/IO/net) and no side effects: it only reads its two
# arguments. Equality is structural and constructor-gated. Recursion is
# unbounded (deeply nested structures recurse); there is NO cycle guard (a
# self-referential input would infinitely recurse / RangeError — but the oracle
# codec cannot encode cycles, so that path is OUT-OF-ENVELOPE, see SCOPE).
#
# An internal helper `find(iter, tar)` exists ONLY to support object-keyed
# Set/Map matching; it is not exported and is described inline below.

# =============================================================================
# EMISSION / INTERFACE CONTRACT (read this, blind re-emitter)
# =============================================================================
# Emit an ES module (.mjs) exposing a NAMED export `dequal`:
#     export function dequal(foo, bar) { ... }
# The grader resolves loadFn(module['dequal']) and calls dequal(foo, bar).
# Return EXACTLY a boolean. Do not return objects/numbers. Do not throw on any
# codec-faithful input (the only throw is unbounded recursion on a cyclic
# input, which the codec cannot produce).
#
# Implementation note for the re-emitter: this is a single function plus a tiny
# internal `find` helper. Reproduce the BRANCH ORDER below EXACTLY — the order
# is observable (e.g. the `foo === bar` short-circuit fires before any
# constructor logic, so two NaN-bearing references that are identity-equal
# return true even though NaN !== NaN).

# =============================================================================
# BEHAVIOR  (exact ordered pipeline — reproduce verbatim)
# =============================================================================
# Let `ctor`, `len`, `tmp` be locals.
#
# NODE 1  — IDENTITY SHORT-CIRCUIT (fires FIRST, before everything):
#     if (foo === bar) return true
#   - Uses JS strict-equality `===`. Note `===` semantics:
#       * 0 === -0  is TRUE  -> dequal(0, -0) returns true HERE (never reaches a
#         sign-aware compare; dequal does NOT distinguish +0 from -0).
#       * NaN === NaN is FALSE -> two DISTINCT NaN values do NOT short-circuit
#         here; they are caught later by NODE 9 (the NaN tail). But the SAME
#         reference passed as both args (identity) returns true here regardless
#         of contents (including a function, a Symbol, a cyclic object, a Date).
#       * Equal primitives of the same type (1===1, ''==='', true===true,
#         Infinity===Infinity, 1n===1n, 'x'==='x') return true here.
#       * Different-type primitives (1 vs '1', '' vs 0, true vs 1, null vs
#         undefined, 0 vs false, 0 vs null) are NOT === -> fall through.
#
# NODE 2  — TRUTHY-OBJECT GATE + CONSTRUCTOR-IDENTITY CHECK:
#     if (foo && bar && (ctor = foo.constructor) === bar.constructor) { ...NODE 3..8... }
#   - `foo && bar` requires BOTH operands truthy. If EITHER is falsy
#     (null, undefined, 0, -0, NaN, '', false), the whole block is SKIPPED and
#     control goes to NODE 9.
#       * Consequence: dequal(null, null) already returned true at NODE 1;
#         dequal(null, {}) -> null falsy -> skip block -> NODE 9 -> false.
#       * dequal(0, false): not === ; 0 falsy -> skip -> NODE 9 -> 0!==0 is false
#         -> returns false.
#   - `ctor = foo.constructor` is assigned, then compared `=== bar.constructor`.
#     If the two constructors are NOT the SAME reference, the block is skipped
#     and control goes to NODE 9 (which yields false for two non-NaN values).
#       * Examples that DIFFER in constructor and thus -> NODE 9 -> false:
#           [] (Array) vs {} (Object); Buffer vs Uint8Array; 1n (BigInt) vs 1
#           (Number); a null-prototype object (ctor === undefined) vs a plain
#           {} (ctor === Object).
#       * For primitives that survived NODE 1 as different VALUES of the same
#         type (2 vs 3): both truthy, both .constructor === Number (same ref) ->
#         ENTERS block, matches none of NODE 3..8, falls to NODE 9 -> 2!==2 false
#         -> returns false. (i.e. unequal same-type numbers are decided by the
#         NaN tail returning false.)
#       * Null-prototype objects: `Object.create(null)` has constructor ===
#         undefined. Two null-proto objects: undefined === undefined is true,
#         so the block IS entered (NODE 8 handles them via the `!ctor` arm).
#
#   Inside the block, branches are tested in THIS EXACT ORDER:
#
# NODE 3  — Date  (SPEC-ONLY; see SCOPE — excluded from oracle):
#     if (ctor === Date) return foo.getTime() === bar.getTime()
#   - Two Dates are equal iff their epoch-millisecond values are strict-equal.
#     (Invalid Date getTime() is NaN, and NaN===NaN is false, so two Invalid
#     Dates compare UNEQUAL here.) NOT exercised by the oracle: the factory
#     codec has no Date tag.
#
# NODE 4  — RegExp:
#     if (ctor === RegExp) return foo.toString() === bar.toString()
#   - CRITICAL: equality is by `RegExp.prototype.toString()`, i.e. the string
#     "/" + source + "/" + flags, where FLAGS ARE IN CANONICAL ORDER. JS
#     normalizes flag order on construction, so /a/ig and /a/gi both toString to
#     "/a/gi". Therefore:
#       * /a/   vs /a/   -> "/a/"   === "/a/"   -> true
#       * /a/g  vs /a/g  -> true
#       * /a/gi vs /a/ig -> both "/a/gi" -> true  (flag-order independent)
#       * /a/g  vs /a/i  -> "/a/g" vs "/a/i" -> false
#       * /a/   vs /b/   -> false
#     Do NOT implement this as raw `source` + raw-input flag concatenation;
#     compare the canonical toString() forms (equivalently: source equal AND the
#     SET of flags equal, since canonical order makes the flag strings equal).
#
# NODE 5  — Array:
#     if (ctor === Array) {
#         if ((len = foo.length) === bar.length) {
#             while (len-- && dequal(foo[len], bar[len]));
#         }
#         return len === -1
#     }
#   - If lengths differ: the inner block is skipped, `len` still holds foo.length
#     (> -1 unless foo was empty). Return `len === -1`. For unequal non-empty
#     lengths this is false. (Empty-vs-empty already handled: foo.length 0, the
#     `if` 0===0 true, the while `0-- ` evaluates `len-- ` => 0 (falsy) so loop
#     body never runs and len becomes -1, return -1===-1 true.)
#   - If lengths equal: iterate from highest index DOWN to 0, recursively
#     dequal each element. The `while (len-- && ...)` decrements len and tests
#     the PRE-decrement value for truthiness AND the recursive result. Loop
#     stops either when a recursive compare returns false (short-circuit) or
#     when len reaches -1 (all elements matched). Return `len === -1`:
#       * len === -1  => every element matched => true.
#       * len > -1    => stopped early on a mismatch => false.
#     Elements compared recursively, so nested arrays/objects/etc. are deep.
#     `undefined` elements: dequal(undefined, undefined) -> NODE 1 true;
#     dequal(undefined, null) -> false. [1,2,3] vs [1,2] -> false (length).
#
# NODE 6  — Set:
#     if (ctor === Set) {
#         if (foo.size !== bar.size) return false
#         for (len of foo) {                  # `len` reused as the member value
#             tmp = len
#             if (tmp && typeof tmp === 'object') {   # object member
#                 tmp = find(bar, tmp)        # find a structurally-equal member in bar
#                 if (!tmp) return false      # no structural match found
#             }
#             if (!bar.has(tmp)) return false
#         }
#         return true
#     }
#   - Sizes must be strict-equal first; differ -> false.
#   - For each member of foo:
#       * PRIMITIVE / falsy member (number, string, bool, null, 0, '', etc., or
#         any non-object): tmp stays the member; require bar.has(tmp) (SameValueZero
#         membership as Set.has uses) else false.
#       * OBJECT (truthy, typeof 'object') member: call find(bar, member) which
#         scans bar's members and returns the FIRST member m for which
#         dequal(m, member) is true (deep structural match), else undefined. If
#         no match (`!tmp`), return false. Otherwise re-bind tmp to that matched
#         member reference and check bar.has(tmp) (true, since tmp came from bar).
#   - Set equality is ORDER-INDEPENDENT (membership-based): {1,2,3} vs {3,2,1}
#     -> true. Object members matched structurally: Set([{k:1}]) vs Set([{k:1}])
#     -> true; Set([{k:1}]) vs Set([{k:2}]) -> false. Mixed primitive+object
#     members supported. Note `find`'s guard returns undefined (falsy) when no
#     match, which the `!tmp` check rejects.
#
# NODE 7  — Map:
#     if (ctor === Map) {
#         if (foo.size !== bar.size) return false
#         for (len of foo) {                  # `len` is the [key, value] entry pair
#             tmp = len[0]                     # key
#             if (tmp && typeof tmp === 'object') {
#                 tmp = find(bar, tmp)         # find structurally-equal KEY in bar
#                 if (!tmp) return false
#             }
#             if (!dequal(len[1], bar.get(tmp))) return false   # compare VALUES
#         }
#         return true
#     }
#   - Sizes must match first.
#   - For each [key, value] entry of foo:
#       * PRIMITIVE key: tmp = key; compare dequal(foo-value, bar.get(key)).
#         If bar lacks the key, bar.get returns undefined; the recursive dequal
#         then decides (e.g. value vs undefined -> false unless value is also
#         undefined). Mismatched value -> false.
#       * OBJECT key (truthy, typeof 'object'): find a structurally-equal key in
#         bar (find iterates bar.keys()); if none, return false; else rebind tmp
#         to the matched key and compare dequal(foo-value, bar.get(tmp)).
#   - Map equality is order-independent on keys; values compared RECURSIVELY
#     (deep). Map([['a',1]]) vs Map([['a',2]]) -> false; Map([['a',{n:[1,2]}]])
#     vs Map([['a',{n:[1,3]}]]) -> false; empty Map vs empty Map -> true.
#
# NODE 7b — find(iter, tar) helper (used by NODE 6 and NODE 7):
#     function find(iter, tar) { for (key of iter.keys()) { if (dequal(key, tar)) return key } }
#   - Iterates iter.keys() (for a Set, keys()===values()===the members; for a
#     Map, keys()===the keys). Returns the first element deep-equal to `tar`,
#     else undefined (implicit). Comparison is via dequal (recursive).
#
# NODE 8  — ArrayBuffer / DataView / typed-array (ArrayBuffer.isView) / plain object:
#     if (ctor === ArrayBuffer) { foo = new Uint8Array(foo); bar = new Uint8Array(bar) }
#     else if (ctor === DataView) {
#         if ((len = foo.byteLength) === bar.byteLength) {
#             while (len-- && foo.getInt8(len) === bar.getInt8(len));
#         }
#         return len === -1
#     }
#     if (ArrayBuffer.isView(foo)) {           # typed arrays (Uint8Array, etc.) + the rewrapped ArrayBuffer case
#         if ((len = foo.byteLength) === bar.byteLength) {
#             while (len-- && foo[len] === bar[len]);
#         }
#         return len === -1
#     }
#     if (!ctor || typeof foo === 'object') {  # plain object / null-proto object
#         len = 0
#         for (ctor in foo) {                  # `ctor` reused as the property-name iterator
#             if (has.call(foo, ctor) && ++len && !has.call(bar, ctor)) return false
#             if (!(ctor in bar) || !dequal(foo[ctor], bar[ctor])) return false
#         }
#         return Object.keys(bar).length === len
#     }
#   - ArrayBuffer (IN-ENVELOPE only if codec emits it — see SCOPE; codec emits
#     Uint8Array/Buffer, not bare ArrayBuffer): rewrap both as Uint8Array, then
#     fall into the isView byte compare.
#   - DataView: SPEC-ONLY (codec has no DataView tag). byteLength compare then
#     reverse signed-byte compare. Excluded from oracle.
#   - TYPED-ARRAY / isView branch (THIS IS IN THE ORACLE — codec round-trips
#     Uint8Array and Node Buffer):
#       * Buffer's constructor is Buffer (a Uint8Array subclass). NODE 2's
#         ctor-identity check means Buffer vs Buffer enters here; Uint8Array vs
#         Uint8Array enters here; Buffer vs Uint8Array have DIFFERENT
#         constructors -> never reach here -> NODE 9 -> false.
#       * Compare byteLength; if equal, reverse element compare foo[len]===bar[len]
#         (strict ===, numeric bytes). Return len === -1 (all bytes matched).
#         Different length -> len > -1 -> false. Empty vs empty -> true.
#       * Uint8Array([1,2,3]) vs Uint8Array([1,2,3]) -> true;
#         Uint8Array([1,2,3]) vs Uint8Array([1,2,4]) -> false;
#         Uint8Array([1,2,3]) vs Uint8Array([1,2]) -> false (length).
#   - PLAIN-OBJECT branch `if (!ctor || typeof foo === 'object')` (THIS IS IN
#     THE ORACLE): reached for plain Objects (ctor===Object) and null-proto
#     objects (ctor===undefined, the `!ctor` arm). Algorithm:
#       * len = 0 (count of foo's OWN enumerable keys actually visited).
#       * for..in over foo (enumerates own + inherited enumerable keys; for the
#         codec's plain objects there are no inherited enumerable keys, so this
#         is effectively foo's own enumerable keys):
#           - First test: `has.call(foo, key) && ++len && !has.call(bar, key)`.
#             If key is foo's OWN property: increment len; if bar does NOT have
#             that own key -> return false. (Note: ++len only happens for own
#             keys; ` && ` short-circuits so for an inherited key len is not
#             bumped.)
#           - Second test: `!(key in bar) || !dequal(foo[key], bar[key])`.
#             If key is not in bar (own OR inherited) OR the recursive value
#             compare fails -> return false.
#       * After the loop, return `Object.keys(bar).length === len`:
#         bar must have EXACTLY as many own-enumerable keys as foo's counted
#         own keys. This is the KEY-COUNT GUARD that makes the comparison
#         symmetric:
#           - {a:1} vs {a:1,b:2}: foo loop counts len=1 and all foo keys match
#             bar; final check Object.keys(bar).length (2) === 1 -> FALSE.
#           - {a:1,b:2} vs {a:1}: foo loop, key 'b' is own (++len) but
#             !has.call(bar,'b') -> return false immediately.
#           - {a:1,b:2} vs {a:1,c:2}: key 'b' own, bar has no own 'b' -> false.
#           - Key order independent: {a:1,b:2} vs {b:2,a:1} -> true.
#           - {a:undefined} vs {a:undefined} -> true (value undefined===undefined
#             via NODE 1). {a:undefined} vs {} -> 'a' own in foo, bar lacks 'a'
#             -> false. {} vs {a:undefined} -> foo empty loop, len=0,
#             Object.keys(bar).length 1 === 0 -> false.
#         Values compared RECURSIVELY (deep nesting, arrays/regex/sets inside).
#
# NODE 9  — NaN TAIL (the final fallthrough return):
#     return foo !== foo && bar !== bar
#   - This is reached when: NODE 1 didn't fire, AND (the NODE-2 block was
#     skipped OR the block was entered but matched none of NODE 3..8 and fell
#     out — e.g. two distinct same-type primitives).
#   - `x !== x` is true ONLY for NaN. So this returns true IFF BOTH foo and bar
#     are NaN:
#       * NaN vs NaN -> true.
#       * NaN vs 1   -> 1!==1 false -> false.
#       * 1 vs 2     -> 1!==1 false -> false.
#       * 1n vs 2n   -> 1n!==1n false -> false.
#       * '' vs 0, null vs {}, [] vs {}, Buffer vs Uint8Array, 1 vs '1' -> false.
#   - Returns a strict boolean.

# =============================================================================
# DECISION SUMMARY (return value for every reachable shape; strict boolean)
# =============================================================================
#   foo === bar (identity, incl. equal same-type primitives, 0/-0)   -> true
#   both NaN                                                          -> true
#   one NaN, other not                                               -> false
#   different-type primitives (1 vs '1', null vs undefined, 0/false)  -> false
#   either operand falsy and not identical (null vs {}, 0 vs null)    -> false
#   different constructors (incl. Buffer vs Uint8Array, BigInt vs Num,
#       null-proto vs plain {}, [] vs {})                             -> false
#   RegExp: canonical toString() equal                               -> true/false
#   Array: same length AND all elements deep-equal                   -> true/false
#   Set: same size AND every member has a deep-equal counterpart      -> true/false
#   Map: same size AND every entry's key+value deep-match             -> true/false
#   typed-array/Buffer: same byteLength AND all bytes ===             -> true/false
#   plain/null-proto object: same own-key COUNT AND all own keys
#       present in both AND all values deep-equal                     -> true/false
#   (Date, DataView, ArrayBuffer: spec-only, see SCOPE)

ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Output is a strict boolean. Distinguishable; recorded directly. No seam.

# =============================================================================
# SCOPE
# =============================================================================
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly; ALL exercised by genInputs, all of
# whose values round-trip the factory codec — JSON primitives/arrays/plain
# objects, RegExp, Set, Map, Uint8Array, Buffer, bigint, undefined, NaN,
# ±Infinity, -0, boxed-free):
#   - NODE 1 identity short-circuit (incl. same-ref, 0/-0, equal primitives,
#     Infinity).
#   - NODE 2 truthy gate + constructor-identity check (incl. differing-ctor
#     and falsy-operand fallthrough).
#   - NODE 4 RegExp (canonical toString()).
#   - NODE 5 Array (length + reverse recursive).
#   - NODE 6 Set (size + structural membership via find).
#   - NODE 7 Map (size + key-match via find + recursive value compare).
#   - NODE 8 typed-array/Buffer byte compare (Uint8Array, Buffer) AND
#     plain-object / null-proto-object key-count + recursive compare.
#   - NODE 9 NaN tail (incl. NaN-vs-NaN true, NaN-vs-number false, distinct
#     same-type primitive false, bigint inequality false).
#
# CARRIED DATA: none. No literal tables. (No carried.json.)
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (NOT emitted by genInputs; the blind
# re-emitter STILL implements the branch from this spec, but the oracle does
# not grade it because the factory codec cannot encode the value):
#   - NODE 3 Date: codec has no Date tag. (Spec preserved above.)
#   - NODE 8 DataView and bare ArrayBuffer: codec has no DataView/ArrayBuffer
#     tag (it ships Uint8Array/Buffer instead). (Spec preserved above.)
#   - Functions, Symbols: not codec-faithful (functions don't survive
#     JSON.stringify(encode(...)); Symbols are identity-only). Behavior IS
#     defined by the spec (a function passed as both args -> NODE 1 identity
#     -> true; two distinct functions -> ctor Function===Function, none of
#     NODE 3..8 match, NODE 9 -> false) but excluded from the generator.
#   - Cyclic / self-referential structures: infinite recursion (no cycle
#     guard). Codec cannot encode cycles; excluded.
#   - undefined / NaN / ±Infinity / -0 as STANDALONE top-level args: these DO
#     round-trip in the factory codec per the decomposer addendum and ARE
#     emitted. (Note: undefined/NaN/±Infinity are NOT placed as array elements
#     or object/Map values unless that specific composite round-trips; the
#     generator keeps them at positions the codec preserves — see generator.)

PROVENANCE dequal@2.0.3 — repo lukeed/dequal — MIT license — source file dist/index.js (CJS dist; the loaded `main`). Verified by executing the installed package across every branch (identity, constructor-dispatch Array/RegExp/Set/Map/typed-array/plain-object, NaN tail, +0/-0, 1-vs-'1', key-count mismatch, different-constructor, bigint, null-proto). Observed returns matched this SIR exactly.
